<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
        <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
        <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            ⚠ **One reference, and it is the whole of the application's plumbing.**
            `Vixen.Ui.Desktop` is the window, the Vulkan device and the four steps of a frame, and it
            brings `Vixen.Ui.Controls` — and therefore the control set, the VXML compiler, the
            `.vxml` and `.vcss` item types and the utility stylesheet step — behind it.

            ⚠ **This used to be five package references and four hundred lines of C#**, because
            everything below `UiApplication` was a file in the template: a swapchain, a render graph,
            an atlas upload, a resize coalescer, a suboptimal-present rule and a font hunter. All of
            it is somebody else's problem now, and the two files left are the two files an
            application is actually about.

            ⚠ **No Vixen.App and no Vixen.Engine, and that absence is still the point.** Vixen.App
            references Vixen.Engine, so taking the convenient host would drag a scene, an ECS world
            and a fixed-step accumulator into an application that is a user interface. What changed
            is that avoiding it is now free rather than four hundred lines.

            ⚠ **And there is nothing here for `AppShell.vxml` or `Theme/vixen.ui.vcss`, which is the
            point rather than an omission.** `Vixen.Ui` carries its MSBuild logic in
            `buildTransitive/`, so the VXML compiler, the two item types and the utility stylesheet
            step all arrive with the reference above. Adding a second `.vxml`, a second `.vcss` or a
            whole folder of them is not a project-file change.

            ⚠ **There is no Vixen.Sdk here either**: an application with no Assets/ folder has
            nothing to import and no content to build, so the SDK would add two no-op build steps and
            a tool dependency for nothing. Add it — `<Project Sdk="Vixen.Sdk/x.y.z">` — the day this
            application starts shipping content.
        -->
        <PackageReference Include="Vixen.Ui.Desktop" Version="VIXEN_PACKAGE_VERSION" />

        <!--
            ⚠ **Hot reload, and this one conditioned line is all of it.** There is no call to make and
            nothing in Program.cs about it: the package's module initializer fills the two hooks
            Vixen.Ui.Desktop leaves open, so this application mounts its content under a reload host
            and watches its own directory for stylesheets. Edit AppShell.vxml under `dotnet watch` and
            the running window updates; edit Theme/vixen.ui.vcss's neighbours and it repaints with no
            rebuild at all.

            ⚠ **Conditioned on Debug, which is what makes it safe to be automatic.** Hot reload reads
            fields by name and is neither trimmable nor AOT-compatible. A Release build does not
            resolve this reference, the initializer never runs, and the hooks stay null — so a shipped
            application carries neither the tool nor an `#if` that mentions it.
        -->
        <PackageReference Include="Vixen.Ui.Desktop.HotReload"
                          Version="VIXEN_PACKAGE_VERSION"
                          Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug'" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
